FADE IN:

EXT. GULL POINT WEATHER STATION - NIGHT

= Establish the station, the storm, and Nell alone at her post.

A squat concrete hut clings to the end of a rocky spit. Wind drives sheets of rain sideways. A single amber window glows against the black.

Above the roof, an anemometer spins so fast it whines.

INT. WEATHER STATION - RADIO ROOM - CONTINUOUS

= Nell logs the storm; a garbled mayday breaks through the static.

Cramped, warm, cluttered. Paper charts pinned three deep. A kettle ticks on a hot plate.

NELL ORTIZ (50s), reading glasses pushed up into grey hair, pencils numbers into a ledger by the light of a gooseneck lamp.

CLOSE ON THE BAROMETER [[shot]]

The needle sags past STORMY and keeps going.

NELL
(to the barometer)
Don't you dare.

She taps the glass. The needle drops another hair.

The RADIO crackles. Static, then a voice, chopped to ribbons.

SKIPPER (V.O.)
(distorted)
...Gull Point, Gull Point... this is the Marguerite... do you read...

Nell lunges for the handset, knocking the ledger to the floor.

NELL
Marguerite, this is Gull Point. I read you. Say your position.

Static. Nell holds her breath.

SKIPPER (V.O.)
...lost the mast... taking water... two souls aboard...

NELL
How many? Say again, how many?

SKIPPER (V.O.)
Two. Me and my boy.

Nell scrawls on the back of a chart: MARGUERITE - 2 - NO MAST.

She flips a switch on a second radio and keys a different handset.

NELL
Coast Guard Sector, Gull Point. I have a vessel in distress, the Marguerite, two aboard, dismasted. Position unknown. I am working on it.

COAST GUARD (V.O.)
Copy, Gull Point. Cutter is forty minutes out in this weather. Get us a position and we'll get them home.

Nell sets the handset down. Forty minutes. She looks at the barometer. Then at the black window.

NELL
(into the radio)
Marguerite, listen to me. I need you to look for a light. Any light. Can you see the lighthouse on Hesper Rock?

A long beat of static.

SKIPPER (V.O.)
I can't see my own hands.

NELL
Then we do it the old way. Is your boy there? Put him on.

SCRATCHING on the line. Then a new voice, young and scared, trying hard not to be.

TEO (V.O.)
This is Teo.

NELL
Teo, I'm Nell. I've been sitting in this little box for twenty-two years, and I've talked a lot of boats home, and every single one of them was in worse shape than yours. You hear me? Every one. So here's what we're going to do. You're going to find the compass on the binnacle, right in front of the wheel. You're going to tell me which way the needle points, and which way the waves are hitting you. Port side or starboard side. Bow or stern. That's all. Just those two things. Can you do that for me?

TEO (V.O.)
...Yeah. Yes.

INT. MARGUERITE - WHEELHOUSE - NIGHT

= Teo reads the compass while his father fights the wheel.

Water sloshes ankle deep. The SKIPPER (60s), a bandage of duct tape around one forearm, wrestles the wheel. TEO (14) clutches the radio with both hands and leans over the swinging compass.

TEO
Needle says... northeast. The waves are coming from the back. The left. Port. Back port.

SKIPPER
Good. Good lad.

INT. WEATHER STATION - RADIO ROOM - NIGHT

Nell drags a ruler across the chart, pencil in her teeth. She plots a line. Another. They cross off the tip of a dotted reef.

NELL
They're north of the reef. They're drifting onto Hesper.

She snatches up both handsets at once.

NELL
Coast Guard, Gull Point.

COAST GUARD (V.O.) ^
Go ahead, Gull Point.

NELL
Two miles north-northwest of Hesper Rock and drifting south. Tell your cutter to come around the lee side.

COAST GUARD (V.O.)
Copy. Two north-northwest of Hesper. Cutter is turning now.

Nell lets out a breath that she seems to have been holding for an hour. She keys the other handset, gentler now.

NELL
Teo. Help is coming. I'm going to stay right here and talk to you until you see their lights. Tell me about your boat.

TEO (V.O.)
(a shaky laugh)
She's ugly.

NELL
The ugly ones float the longest.

Nell picks the ledger up off the floor, smooths the page, and writes the time.

The kettle begins, at last, to whistle.

>FADE OUT.
